#{[17592186317550 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=(8x+4){e}^{2x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2[{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(8x+4){e}^{2x}\\cdot 2}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int (8x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317551 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-{sec}^{3}⁡(x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(-{sec}^{3}⁡(x))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320790 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and $‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321314 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186321315 :answer/value "-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186321738 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186321771 :answer/value "4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186321836 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186321987 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322020 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322285 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322306 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322338 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322355 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322482 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322489 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322635 :answer/value "-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322717 :answer/value "5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322718 :answer/value "-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322723 :answer/value "5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322839 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186322884 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186323144 :answer/value "-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186323745 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & ={z}_{11}{C}_{11}+{z}_{12}{C}_{12}+{z}_{13}{C}_{13}+{z}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{11}-{C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & ∗ & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix})-(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-31) \\\\  & =-62\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323746 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & ={u}_{11}{C}_{11}+{u}_{12}{C}_{12}+{u}_{13}{C}_{13}+{u}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {C}_{11}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =5{C}_{11}-2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & ∗ & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & ∗ & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix})+2(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =5(0-(6\\cdot (-4)-1\\cdot 2)+0)+2(0-(3\\cdot (-4)-1\\cdot (-3))+0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 26+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =130+18 \\\\  & =148\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323769 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186323769 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+4\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323891 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+18) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial  $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"] [17592186323940 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-4A+I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-4A+I) & =O \\\\ A-4I+{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =4I-A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\n$$\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n$$"] [17592186323945 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323946 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+5) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"] [17592186323974 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186323974 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(32-0)+1(0-0)+2(0-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323975 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186323975 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(4-0)-0+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186324045 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186324479 :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186324505 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325095 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325369 :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325475 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325812 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325933 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186325994 :answer/value "-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186326210 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186326669 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186326669 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(-20-0)-0+3(0-5) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-20)+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =20-15 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326670 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186326670 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+7\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(-2-0)-0+7(0-2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-2)+7\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-12-14 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326671 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$E$ is a singular matrix” is true."] [17592186326672 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186326672 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+1(0-6)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6-6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326673 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(8-14)+6(5-8) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$Q$ is a singular matrix” is true."] [17592186326707 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326708 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186326811 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326812 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-5A-2 & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-5A-2) & ={A}^{-1}O \\\\ A-5I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{5}{2},$ $q=\\frac{1}{2}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-2.\n$$"] [17592186326819 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 4)-3(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 4)+0 \\\\  & =25+6 \\\\  & =31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|31|=31.\n$$"] [17592186326843 :question/worked-solution "First, to get the minor of ${a}_{13}$,  we remove the $1$st row and the $3$rd column:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 1 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 2 & ∗ & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & ∗ & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & ∗ & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & ∗ & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{13}=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cofactor of ${a}_{13}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}{M}_{13} \\\\  & ={M}_{13} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326925 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)-2(3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2k-2+6 \\\\  & =-2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ -2k+4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$"] [17592186326926 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 1)+2(2\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 1)+(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-22+20+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|2|=2.\n$$"] [17592186326927 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$"] [17592186326927 :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+1\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326932 :question/problem "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{vmatrix}=-5.\n$$"] [17592186326932 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have  a block upper triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =|\\begin{array}{cc|c}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326979 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186326998 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,13 & 7 \\\\ 19 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186327020 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 10 \\\\ -7 & -14\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186327065 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186327131 :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-46 & 8 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}"] [17592186327185 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186327186 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186327226 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}."] [17592186327465 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -11 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 & \\,\\,11\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,-2 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186327491 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}13 & \\,\\,\\,4 \\\\ 19 & -2\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}\\,\\,\\,4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186327566 :answer/value "\\begin{